THE LATTICE LEDGER
Higher-ed technology, read so you don't have to
Week of 1 March 2027 | Issue 112

Good morning. Three things this week: buyers are getting pickier, a
survey says what everyone suspected, and a guest columnist wants you to
stop arguing about chargeback until you have argued about something
else first.

- Lorne Castellan, Editor


1. THE "PILOT FOREVER" ERA MAY BE ENDING

Procurement officers we spoke to this month describe the same shift: AI
tools that slipped in through free pilots and departmental credit cards
are now being pulled back into formal purchasing. The trigger is rarely
a scandal. It is usually an audit finding, or a renewal invoice that
lands on someone who never knew the pilot existed.

What buyers are asking for now, in rough order of frequency:
  - written terms on whether student work is used to train models;
  - accessibility conformance documentation before a demo, not after;
  - a named support path once the vendor's onboarding team moves on;
  - pricing that does not quietly double when a second campus joins.

One procurement director put it bluntly: "If the answer to 'who
supports this in year two?' is a shrug, we stop there."


2. SURVEY: MOST INSTITUTIONS CAN'T SAY WHAT THEY SPEND ON AI

A consortium survey of 140 public institutions, circulated to members
last week, found that only about one in five could produce a single
figure for annual spending on AI tools. The rest pointed to spending
spread across departmental budgets, IT subscriptions and research
grants.

Other findings:
  - 61% have at least one AI tool in use that central IT did not
    procure;
  - 38% have a written policy on student data in AI tools, up from 24%
    a year ago;
  - fewer than 10% have retired a pilot on purpose.

The survey authors caution that self-reporting likely flatters the
numbers. We suspect they are right.


3. OP-ED: CHARGEBACK IS A CONVERSATION ABOUT TRUST, NOT ARITHMETIC

Our guest columnist this week, a former budget officer at a multi-campus
system, argues that cost-sharing models for shared platforms fail for
social reasons long before they fail for mathematical ones.

"Every chargeback model I have seen - consumption-based, flat fee,
hybrid - was defensible on a spreadsheet," she writes. "The ones that
survived were the ones where the smallest member believed the formula
had been written with them in the room. The ones that failed were
presented as finished."

Her advice: show the options early, explain who wins and who loses
under each, and accept that the first year's numbers will be wrong.
"Nobody remembers that your year-one estimate was off by eleven
percent. Everybody remembers that they found out about the model from
a slide."

Agree? Disagree? Reply to this email; we print the best responses.


